{
  "text": "```verilog\nmodule areset_deassert_sync #(\n    parameter CHAINS = 2,\n    parameter RST_POL = 1'b1\n)(\n    input  clk,\n    input  async_rst_i,\n    output sync_rst_o\n);\n\n    localparam DEPTH = (CHAINS > 0) ? CHAINS : 1;\n\n    reg [DEPTH-1:0] sync_reg;\n\n    // Active reset value: 1 if active-high, 0 if active-low\n    localparam ACTIVE_VAL  = RST_POL;\n    localparam INACTIVE_VAL = ~RST_POL;\n\n    // Synchronize the release of the async reset into the clk domain.\n    // On assertion, the output should follow async_rst_i immediately (async assert).\n    // On deassertion, the output should be released synchronously to clk.\n\n    // We use a shift register to synchronize the deassertion.\n    // The idea: \n    //   - If async_rst_i is active, sync_rst_o should be active (asserted).\n    //   - If async_rst_i is inactive, we need to wait for the synchronized signal to become inactive before deasserting.\n    //\n    // A common approach:\n    //   - Use a chain of flip-flops to synchronize async_rst_i.\n    //   - The output is active if either the async input is active OR the synchronized chain hasn't yet cleared.\n    //\n    // More precisely:\n    //   - sync_reg[0] <= async_rst_i (first stage)\n    //   - sync_reg[i] <= sync_reg[i-1] for i > 0\n    //   - The synchronized reset is active if async_rst_i is active OR any bit in the chain is still active.\n    //   - Actually, we want: assert immediately when async_rst_i asserts, and deassert only when the entire chain has cleared.\n    //\n    // Let's think about it differently:\n    //   - We want sync_rst_o to be active (asserted) when:\n    //     1. async_rst_i is currently active, OR\n    //     2. async_rst_i was recently active and the synchronization chain hasn't fully cleared yet.\n    //\n    // The chain should track the \"active\" state. When async_rst_i goes inactive, the chain will shift in inactive values,\n    // and after DEPTH cycles, all bits will be inactive.\n    //\n    // So: sync_rst_o = async_rst_i | sync_reg[DEPTH-1] (if we use the last stage as the \"fully synchronized\" indicator)\n    // But wait, we need to be careful with polarity.\n    //\n    // Let's define:\n    //   - rst_active = (async_rst_i == ACTIVE_VAL)\n    //   - We synchronize rst_active into the chain.\n    //   - sync_rst_o should be ACTIVE_VAL if (rst_active | sync_reg[DEPTH-1]) else INACTIVE_VAL.\n    //\n    // Actually, let's simplify by working with the active sense:\n    //   - Let active_in = (async_rst_i == ACTIVE_VAL)\n    //   - Synchronize active_in through the chain.\n    //   - The output is active if active_in OR the last stage of the chain is active.\n    //   - Then convert back to the correct polarity.\n\n    wire active_in = (async_rst_i == ACTIVE_VAL);\n\n    integer i;\n    always @(posedge clk) begin\n        sync_reg[0] <= active_in;\n        for (i = 1; i < DEPTH; i = i + 1)\n            sync_reg[i] <= sync_reg[i-1];\n    end\n\n    wire active_out = active_in | sync_reg[DEPTH-1];\n\n    assign sync_rst_o = active_out ? ACTIVE_VAL : INACTIVE_VAL;\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>",
